{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Horizon-dependent bias: forecasts are slightly high in the first ~1–10 days (typically +0.3 to +2 €/t), but become materially too low beyond ~20 days when the market trends up (often -2 to -10 €/t). Apply a bias that shifts from negative (reduce forecast) at short horizons to positive (increase forecast) at long horizons when recent momentum is positive.",
    "Underreaction to sustained uptrends: when the last 5–10 days show a clear upward drift, the model’s path rises too slowly and tops out too early. Add a momentum/trend continuation term that increases with horizon (e.g., scale by recent slope) to avoid long-horizon underestimation.",
    "Over-mean-reversion after rallies: the model tends to pull prices back toward mid/high-60s after a spike, while realized prices often keep grinding higher. Reduce mean-reversion strength when prices are making higher highs and higher lows (trend regime filter).",
    "Peak/trough timing lag: the model often predicts local peaks earlier than realized (i.e., it ‘rolls over’ too soon). Shift turning points later by a few days when volatility is elevated and trend is positive.",
    "Volatility compression: forecast range is narrower than realized during strong moves (misses upside tail). Inflate forecast variance (wider band / higher drift) when recent realized volatility and positive momentum are both high."
  ]
}
